<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
    What leaves the device when the user backs up to the cloud or moves to a new phone.

    The policy is "restore the library, not the bytes": everything that represents a decision the
    user made is backed up, and everything that is large or that only makes sense on the device that
    produced it is left behind.

    Stated as exclusions rather than inclusions on purpose. With no <include>, the platform backs up
    the app's files, databases and shared preferences by default, so anything added later — a new
    Room table, another DataStore — is protected without anyone remembering to come back here. The
    two exclusions below are the things that would actively be wrong to carry over.

    Not listed because the platform already excludes them: cacheDir (Coil's image_cache and OkHttp's
    http cache both live there) and no_backup/.

    Backed up, deliberately:
      - databases/bpodcat.db — subscriptions, episodes, listening positions and the queue. This is
        the whole point; it is small and it is not recoverable from anywhere else.
      - files/datastore/bpodcat_preferences.preferences_pb — playback speed, skip intervals,
        auto-download settings. Small, and every value in it is a choice the user made.

    There is no backup_rules.xml counterpart: `android:fullBackupContent` applies only to API 30 and
    below, and minSdk is 34.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <!--
            The downloaded audio: gigabytes, and re-downloadable from the feed. Sending it to cloud
            backup would blow through the per-app quota and take the rest of the backup with it.
        -->
        <exclude domain="file" path="episode_downloads" />
        <!--
            Media3's own index of what is downloaded and cached. It must not travel without the
            audio files above, or the restored app shows episodes as downloaded that are not on
            disk. Media3 rebuilds it from the (empty) cache directory on first use.
        -->
        <exclude domain="database" path="exoplayer_internal.db" />
        <exclude domain="database" path="exoplayer_internal.db-wal" />
        <exclude domain="database" path="exoplayer_internal.db-shm" />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <!-- Same reasoning as above; a direct transfer is size-limited too, and the index would be
             just as wrong without the files it indexes. -->
        <exclude domain="file" path="episode_downloads" />
        <exclude domain="database" path="exoplayer_internal.db" />
        <exclude domain="database" path="exoplayer_internal.db-wal" />
        <exclude domain="database" path="exoplayer_internal.db-shm" />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
